// Test: the positional family has no meaning on a dictionary — decision 11.
//
// This file used to be "$[start:count] and $-[start:count] on named tuples" and
// asserted that a dictionary could be sliced and trimmed by position. The whole
// family went with the withdrawal of `d[2]`, because the reasoning is the same
// one: in a mutable dictionary a position is not a stable address — adding a key
// changes what sits at each one. There is no principled line between "the second
// key" and "the first two keys", and a positional WRITE (`d[2]$~ v`, `d$-[2]`)
// is strictly worse than a positional read, since it corrupts data rather than
// returning the wrong value.
//
// This is Python's position: `dict` has no indexing and no slicing. The slice
// gets NO key-based replacement, and that is deliberate — "the first two keys"
// is not a question a dictionary should answer.
//
// The refusals are pinned in reject/collections/02. What this file keeps is the
// half that survives: the same operators on an ARRAY and on a POSITIONAL tuple,
// where the index is the only address there is and the size is fixed.

// ── Array: the whole family still works ─────────────────────────────────────
arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
>> arr$[1:2] ¶
>> arr$[1..2] ¶
>> arr$[2:2] ¶
>> arr$[2..3] ¶
>> arr$-[2] ¶
>> arr$-[1:2] ¶
>> arr$-[1..2] ¶
>> arr[2] ¶
>> (arr[2]$~ 99) ¶

// ── Positional tuple: the same, and the index is its only address ───────────
t = (10, 20, 30, 40, 50)
>> t$[1:2] ¶
>> t$[2..3] ¶
>> t$-[2] ¶
>> t[2] ¶
>> (t[2]$~ 99) ¶

// ── Dictionary: addressed by key, and only by key ───────────────────────────
d = #(id: 1, name: "Ana", age: 25)
>> d["id"] ¶
>> d["name"] ¶
>> (d["age"]$~ 26) ¶
>> (d$-["name"]) ¶
>> (d$? "id") ¶
>> d$# ¶
